1. Salesforce — SOQL, Collections & Test Classes | ~55 min

Day 1 ka golden pattern yaad rakho:

Collect IDs → Query once → Map data → Process → DML once

Suppose requirement hai:

> Jab Contacts update hon, unke related Accounts fetch karo aur sirf Active Accounts par processing karo.



Bad Apex:

for (Contact con : Trigger.new) {
    Account acc = [
        SELECT Id, Name, Status__c
        FROM Account
        WHERE Id = :con.AccountId
    ];
}

200 Contacts aaye → potentially 200 queries. Governor limit hit.

Better approach:

Set<Id> accountIds = new Set<Id>();

for (Contact con : Trigger.new) {
    if (con.AccountId != null) {
        accountIds.add(con.AccountId);
    }
}

Map<Id, Account> accountMap = new Map<Id, Account>([
    SELECT Id, Name, Status__c
    FROM Account
    WHERE Id IN :accountIds
]);

for (Contact con : Trigger.new) {
    Account acc = accountMap.get(con.AccountId);

    if (acc != null && acc.Status__c == 'Active') {
        // business logic
    }
}

Three collections samjho:

Collection	Best use

List<T>	Ordered records, duplicates allowed
Set<T>	Unique values/IDs
Map<K,V>	Key-based fast lookup


Example:

List<String> names = new List<String>{
    'Rachel', 'Rachel', 'Alex'
};

3 values.

Set<String> names = new Set<String>{
    'Rachel', 'Rachel', 'Alex'
};

Effectively:

Rachel
Alex

Now:

Map<Id, Account> accountMap =
    new Map<Id, Account>(accounts);

Then instead of repeatedly searching a List:

Account acc = accountMap.get(accountId);

This is why Maps are extremely common in trigger handlers.


---

Relationship SOQL

Instead of separate queries unnecessarily, Salesforce relationships can help.

Child → Parent:

SELECT Id,
       FirstName,
       LastName,
       Account.Name,
       Account.Industry
FROM Contact

You can access:

con.Account.Name

Parent → Children:

SELECT Id,
       Name,
       (SELECT Id, FirstName, LastName
        FROM Contacts)
FROM Account

Then:

for (Account acc : accounts) {

    for (Contact con : acc.Contacts) {
        System.debug(con.LastName);
    }
}

Interview question:

SOQL vs SOSL?

A good short answer:

> SOQL is used when I know which object/data structure I need and want to query records using specific fields and conditions. SOSL is useful for text-based searches across multiple objects and fields.



Example SOQL:

SELECT Id, Name
FROM Account
WHERE Industry = 'Banking'

SOSL:

FIND 'Rachel*'
IN ALL FIELDS
RETURNING
    Contact(Id, Name),
    Lead(Id, Name)


---

Governor Limits you should know

Don't try memorising every Salesforce limit.

For interviews, at minimum know the important transaction limits conceptually and the commonly tested synchronous limits:

SOQL queries: 100

DML statements: 150

And understand why governor limits exist:

Salesforce is a multi-tenant platform, so limits prevent one transaction/customer's code from monopolising shared platform resources.

Much better interview answer than simply saying:

> “Salesforce only allows 100 SOQL.”




