Apex Test Classes

Now important part.

Suppose:

public class AccountService {

    public static void activateAccount(Id accountId) {

        Account acc = [
            SELECT Id, Status__c
            FROM Account
            WHERE Id = :accountId
        ];

        acc.Status__c = 'Active';

        update acc;
    }
}

Test:

@IsTest
private class AccountServiceTest {

    @IsTest
    static void shouldActivateAccount() {

        Account acc = new Account(
            Name = 'Test Account'
        );

        insert acc;

        Test.startTest();

        AccountService.activateAccount(acc.Id);

        Test.stopTest();

        Account result = [
            SELECT Status__c
            FROM Account
            WHERE Id = :acc.Id
        ];

        System.assertEquals(
            'Active',
            result.Status__c
        );
    }
}

The point of a test is not merely coverage.

A strong interview answer:

> I write Apex tests to validate business behaviour, positive and negative scenarios, bulk processing and expected outcomes. Code coverage is required for deployment, but coverage alone doesn't prove that the implementation works correctly.



That's a very important distinction.

Test.startTest() / Test.stopTest()

These give the code-under-test a fresh governor-limit context and Test.stopTest() causes queued asynchronous work created during the test context—such as future/queueable work—to execute before assertions where applicable.

So:

// test data setup

Test.startTest();

// actual operation being tested

Test.stopTest();

// assertions

Good mental structure:

Arrange → Act → Assert

Arrange
Create test data

Act
Execute functionality

Assert
Verify result


---

@TestSetup

If multiple tests require common data:

@IsTest
private class OpportunityServiceTest {

    @TestSetup
    static void createData() {

        Account acc = new Account(
            Name = 'Test Account'
        );

        insert acc;
    }

    @IsTest
    static void testScenarioOne() {
        // ...
    }

    @IsTest
    static void testScenarioTwo() {
        // ...
    }
}

This reduces repeated setup.

One more interview point:

Avoid relying on real org data.

Normally:

@IsTest

runs with data isolation (SeeAllData=false behaviour).

Your tests should create their own required data.

That makes tests portable across Dev → QA → SIT → Production.

Given you've dealt with behaviour differing between environments, this is especially worth understanding: environment-independent tests are a design goal, not just a deployment checkbox.

