2. Core Java — Collections | ~25 min

This connects nicely with Apex.

ArrayList

List<String> developers = new ArrayList<>();

developers.add("Rachel");
developers.add("Alex");
developers.add("Rachel");

Duplicates allowed.

Conceptually:

Rachel
Alex
Rachel

HashSet

Set<String> developers = new HashSet<>();

developers.add("Rachel");
developers.add("Alex");
developers.add("Rachel");

Duplicates aren't retained.

Conceptually:

Rachel
Alex

Don't depend on HashSet iteration order.

HashMap

Map<Integer, String> employees = new HashMap<>();

employees.put(101, "Rachel");
employees.put(102, "Alex");

System.out.println(
    employees.get(101)
);

Output:

Rachel

Compare:

Java                   Apex

ArrayList              List
HashSet                Set
HashMap                 Map

The APIs aren't identical, but the mental model transfers very well.

Common interview question

ArrayList vs LinkedList?

ArrayList uses a dynamic-array structure and is generally strong for indexed access.

LinkedList is node-based and can make insert/remove operations at known positions/ends convenient, but random indexed access requires traversal.

Don't answer:

> LinkedList is always faster for insertion.



That's oversimplified. Real performance depends on where you're inserting and whether you already have the node/iterator position.

HashMap question

What happens here?

Map<String, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();

map.put("A", 10);
map.put("A", 20);

Final value:

A → 20

Because keys are unique and the second put replaces the value for "A".

